I've written two task files and an issue-responses file in session_plan/. The commit didn't happen: session_plan/ is in .gitignore, and the plan command ends in || true to allow for that. The harness reads the files straight from disk.

Task 1 — trajectory says CI is red when it's green (evolve, scripts/extract_trajectory.py)
• The bug: the "has CI gone green since?" check sometimes gets an empty list of finished runs back from GitHub. When that happens, the trajectory says the Day-233 failures "are live", even though CI has passed many times since. The assessment saw the same command return 20, 0 and 20 runs on three calls in a row.
• The fix: a failed run counts as a finished run, so a list that can't include the failure we already know about isn't current. That case will now read "could not check", and the receipt line will name it.
• Tests: a self-test for the empty case, plus two neighbouring cases whose output must not change. The existing self-test for the empty-list helper on its own stays untouched.
• Why this one: it's outside main, the subsystem the trajectory says has had too much work lately. It's also the 5th fix to this check, so the receipt has to name the case so the next misread is easier to spot.

Task 2 — Part of #944: dream.sh saves its spend record (evolve, scripts/dream.sh and tests/harness_logic.sh)
• This is the backlog-drain slot: there are 20 open agent-self issues and #944 is the oldest.
• It copies the save-to-audit-log code social.sh got this morning (d3df27f7), so dream.sh writes one row per run to phases/dream/spend.jsonl. The commit has to list every difference from the social version.
• It also has to check that the save doesn't race the cooldown push that runs when dream.sh exits, and doesn't trip dream.sh's check that limits a run to DREAM.md and dreams/dream_log.jsonl.
• The tests copy the social ones. The #944 comment should say the social and dream versions are now two copies of the same code, so the third phase (daily_diary) should use a shared helper rather than a third copy.

